# Mario Double DQN

A resume-ready reinforcement learning project that trains an AI agent to play
Super Mario Bros with a Double Deep Q-Network. The implementation follows the
spirit of the official PyTorch Mario RL tutorial while organizing the code like
a real project: CLI commands, configs, checkpoints, metric logs, plots, tests,
and a clear write-up.

> Assignment goal: implement an AI-powered Mario agent using Double Deep
> Q-Networks that can play the game by itself.

## Highlights

- Double DQN agent with online and target convolutional Q-networks.
- Experience replay buffer for stable off-policy learning.
- Atari-style preprocessing: frame skipping, grayscale resize, and frame stack.
- Config-driven training for quick CPU smoke runs or longer GPU/Colab runs.
- CSV metric logging, checkpoint saving, evaluation, and plot regeneration.
- Lightweight tests for the core project pieces.

## Environment

The Mario emulator stack is older and is most reliable on Python 3.10 or 3.11.
This repo intentionally declares `>=3.10,<3.12`. If your system only has Python
3.13, install Python 3.11 first, then create the environment with that version.

```powershell
py -3.11 -m venv .venv
.\.venv\Scripts\Activate.ps1
python -m pip install --upgrade pip
python -m pip install -e ".[dev]"
```

## Usage

Run a short CPU-friendly smoke training job:

```powershell
mario-dqn train --config configs/smoke.toml
```

Run the full training configuration:

```powershell
mario-dqn train --config configs/train.toml
```

Evaluate a saved checkpoint:

```powershell
mario-dqn eval --config configs/smoke.toml --checkpoint runs/smoke/<run-id>/checkpoints/latest.pt
```

Regenerate metric plots from a run directory:

```powershell
mario-dqn plot --run-dir runs/smoke/<run-id>
```

Run tests:

```powershell
pytest
```

## How Double DQN Works

Classic DQN learns an action-value function `Q(s, a)` that estimates the future
return from taking an action in a state. It can overestimate action values
because the same network both chooses and evaluates the best next action.

Double DQN reduces that bias by splitting the work:

- the online network selects the best next action;
- the target network evaluates that action;
- the target network is synced from the online network periodically.

The agent also uses epsilon-greedy exploration: early training is mostly random,
then it gradually shifts toward the best actions predicted by the network.

## Project Structure

```text
src/mario_dqn/
  agent.py       Double DQN agent, action selection, learning, checkpoints
  cli.py         train/eval/plot command-line interface
  config.py      typed TOML config loading
  env.py         Super Mario environment factory and wrappers
  logging.py     CSV metrics and checkpoint directory management
  model.py       convolutional Q-network
  plots.py       reward/loss/Q-value chart generation
  replay.py      experience replay buffer
  train.py       training loop
  evaluate.py    evaluation loop
```

## Resume Bullets

- Built a Double DQN reinforcement learning agent in PyTorch to play Super Mario
  Bros using frame-stacked visual observations and experience replay.
- Engineered a reusable RL training pipeline with config files, checkpoints,
  metric logging, evaluation, and plot generation.
- Implemented CPU-friendly smoke tests and documented a reproducible setup for
  older Gym/NES emulator dependencies.

## Results Policy

This project should only claim strong gameplay performance after a real long
training run has produced checkpoint metrics or a demo video. The smoke config
exists to verify the pipeline quickly; it is not expected to train a good Mario
agent.

